From 3ce44c22485741ed506d24461170203dc775ca88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:14:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 31/61] headless-parity

A headless browser reports what a headed one does. Its screen
(--screen-info) is taken from the primary monitor -- pixels, work area,
system scale, internal or external as the display path says -- unless a
screen is given outright, and its window is maximized on it
(--start-maximized) with a maximized window's frame: headless zooms a
maximized window to the work area without Windows' outdent, so the window
handler gives it no client insets. A page comparing screen, window and
viewport sees a maximized window on a real desktop rather than headless's
800x600 screen under a bigger window, or a window filling the work area with
a restored window's borders. Applied after the seed-derived screen, which
therefore stands when present. That is on Windows; on Linux, a build
presenting Windows has no Windows monitor to copy, so its headless screen is
one fixed Windows desktop: 1920x1080 at a scale of 1 with a 48 px taskbar
along the bottom, the window maximized on it. Initial focus goes to the page
rather than the location bar, which nobody types in headless, so
document.hasFocus() is true as in a window someone is using; a protocol
navigation never moves focus by itself. On Linux a restored window's frame
is Windows' as well: 8 px at the sides and the bottom, which a page reads as
outerWidth minus innerWidth, and none when maximized or fullscreen.
